Original commit body from 03a9b9d6f2dce6c8479e0adcc67a98576173fc56:

ADR-0050 section 1's axiom ("a boundary value names what data crosses the
process boundary at this call site") was applied three ways in the
catalogues: by MEDIUM (logging "names a medium"), by CONTENT (env_read
"values that may carry a credential", document.cookie as env_read), and by
CHOOSER (navigation_read, "a value the party that navigated chose"). The
owner endorsed the medium reading (2026-10-22) and ruled its exact form
(2026-10-25, via WI-zidam's cookie route):

  A row's primary boundary names the MEDIUM; a `simultaneous` second
  boundary may record the reading a taint label needs (credential-bearing;
  chosen by the party that navigated), as the shipped INV-dadu URL rows
  already do. It must state outright that env_read therefore still means
  both the process environment and "may carry a credential".

Rewritten in place, no addendum (docs/adr/README.md lifecycle):
* ADR-0050 section 1: the axiom in that form; env_read's two readings stated
  outright (primary = the process environment: env vars and the ambient
  configuration a deployment supplies; wherever declared = may carry a
  credential, hence host_secret); INV-tutar's split restated on the medium
  (host description and identity come from the host). The claim that the
  axiom was "taken verbatim from ADR-0049 ruling 1" was false -- ruling 1 is
  the transfer test, a different sentence -- and now says the axiom applies
  that test to the axis.
* io_boundary_types: the module's axiom, and the env_read, host_info_read
  and navigation_read definitions, which were the content / identity /
  chooser readings the premise check found (navigation_read's now also says
  env_read is the URL rows' scalar io_boundary by declaration order).
* scripts/generate-concept-axes' hard-coded axiom and data_crossing text;
  docs/concept-axes.md regenerated.
* elixir.yaml / erlang.yaml Application.get_env notes asserted the opposite
  of the ruling ("env_read means 'may carry a credential', NOT 'reads the
  process environment'"); they now say application config is the
  environment's medium and may carry a credential. No boundary changes.

